artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a living system modeled as a coupled dynamical system whose state variables include (i) a substrate of physical memory structures M (e.g., behavioral engrams encoded in neural connectivity, electrophysiological patterns across cells, or genomic/epigenomic sequences), (ii) a body/architecture B that may undergo radical remodeling (e.g., destruction and rebuilding of neural tissue during metamorphosis, or large-scale tissue regeneration), and (iii) a controller/agent process A that reads from M to produce goal-directed behavior in B over time. An interpretation map I: M × B → Behavior decodes memories into actions in the current bodily context, and the system pursues forward-looking goals G specified by anticipated future states.

Identify a minimal set of dynamical conditions on the triple (M, B, A) and on the interpretation map I that are simultaneously necessary and sufficient for the following property to hold: across episodes of radical structural change of B (with continuity of identity and partial preservation of M), the agent continues to creatively re-interpret M—possibly via novel mappings I' not used before the remodeling—so as to advance forward-looking goals G in the new bodily context. Formally characterize:

(a) the information-theoretic and dynamical invariants of M that must be preserved under remodeling of B;
(b) the closure or self-referential properties of A required for it to generate new interpretation maps I' rather than only fixed decodings;
(c) the coupling (in particular, the role of bioelectric / electrophysiological dynamics across all cells, not only excitable nerve and muscle cells) between M, B, and A that is necessary and sufficient for goal-directed creative reinterpretation across major morphological transitions.

Provide a proof of necessity and sufficiency, or rigorous counterexamples delimiting which conditions can be weakened.

